ility of an  mhc-1 mediated  immune response leading to the  destruction of gene-edited cells  in addition a large fraction of the  human population has  pre-existing immunity to aav and would  be ineligible for an aav based therapy  and finally aav has a fairly small  packaging size limit and if you're  trying to accomplish something like  homology directed dna repair  that requires using two aav delivery  vectors  one for the cas9 and the other for the  guide rna and the donor dna  and this can only be achieved in animal  models if you use  extremely high doses of aav which are  generally far beyond what is clinically  acceptable  there is therefore great interest in  developing non-viral methods for  delivering the cas9  rnp non-viral delivered cast 9 has the  benefit  that the cast 9 being delivered will be  rapidly degraded  within 24 hours after delivery and that  avoids the two major problems associated  with viruses  the because the cas9 is transient the  amount of off-target dna damage should  get dramatically  minimized in addition because the cas9  is transient the possibility of an mhc-1  mediated immune response  also pretty much goes away  um cast 9 has been delivered non-virally  in three different formats aya  either as by a plasmid mrna or as the  protein itself  and a variety of nanoparticle-based  technologies have been developed to do  this  however in general non-viral based cast  iron delivery is orders of magnitude  less efficient  than virally based cas9 delivery and is  therefore great interest in developing  new types of non-viral delivery  strategies  for the cas9 rnp  to address this unmet need our  laboratory has developed a few different  strategies for delivering the cas9 rnp  the first one i'll talk about is a  nanoparticle technology called crispr  gold  crisper gold is schematically shown on  this slide here  and crispr gold is composed of  a gold nanoparticle core that has  a donor dna conjugated to it and  absorbed onto all this  is the cas9 rnp that's schematically  shown here  and in addition to having the cas9 rnp  and the donor dna crispr gold also has  an endosomal disruptive polymer  called psdet which has two different  functions it stimulates endocytosis and  also disrupt endosomes  so crispr gold is designed to deliver  all the components needed for gene  correction by hdr  in particular cas9 guide rna and donor  dna in one nanoparticle  crispr gold has numerous potential  applications  uh the one we focused on originally was  uh using it for treating a disease  called duchene's muscular dystrophy  duchenne's is a genetic disease that has  a frequency of about one in three  thousand five hundred  among caucasian males so it's a fairly  uh common genetic diseases  um it only affects males and there's no  cure for this disease and everybody who  gets this disease  um has a lifespan that only uh goes up  to about the 30s or the 40 so there's a  tremendous  unmet medical need in the case of  duchesne's muscular dystrophy  duchenne's muscular dystrophy is caused  by a mutation in the dystrophin protein  that's schematically shown here  dystrophin is uh  is a protein that is necessary for  muscle tissue to stay healthy  and induce and in patients with dmd this  this protein is mutated  and therefore patients with dmd their  muscle tissue starts to waste away  and that eventually causes death by by  suffocation  now cast iron-based therapeutics have  the potential to treat  duchenne's muscular dystrophy by cutting  the dystrophin gene at the mutation site  and correcting it by a homology directed  dna repair  so we therefore investigated if crispr  gold could do this and we did this in  collaboration with irena convoy's lab  who's also a professor  at uc berkeley and a muscle biologist  so the experiment we did was we made  crispr gold that had cast iron protein  and guide rna and donor dna that was  designed to correct the dystrophin  mutation  and we injected it into mice and  investigated if we could get  hdr in the correction of the dystrophin  gene for these experiments we use the  mdx mouse model the mdx mouse is a  transgenic mouse  that has a c to t mutation in the  dystrophin gene  in exon 21 and this generates a stop  codon so  mdx mice do not make dystrophin similar  to  dmd patients so we made crispr gold  designed to correct this mutation  injected it into mdx mice in their  muscle tissue  isolated the muscle tissue and then did  deep sequencing to see  if we could correct the dystrophin gene  the mutated dystrophin gene back to the  wild type sequence  and we analyze this by deep sequencing  the results are shown here the y-axis is  the hdr efficiency the x-axis  is the various groups and what you can  see is that the mice treated with crispr  gold their muscle tissue  had a five percent uh uh conversion rate  to the wild-type gene so in the dmd mice  that in the mdx mice that were treated  with crispr gold  in the in the vicinity of the injection  five 5.4 percent of the mutated gene had  been corrected back to the wild type  sequence  the control which is the cas9 rnp uh  plus donor dna had essentially  background levels  5 is actually a reasonably good number  uh in the case of  of genetic diseases like duchenne's it's  predicted that would be around where  you would expect to see some clinical  benefits  we also analyzed these mice for the  re-expression of dystrophin  protein via immunohistochemistry so we  injected these mice with crispr gold  made histology slides and stained with a  dystrophin antibody the results are  shown here  uh wild-type mice uh have abundant  dystrophin uh  immunostaining so each one of these  little circles here  is is a muscle fiber that's staining for  dystrophin  the control mice the control mdx mice uh  which have been treated with nothing  have no  evidence of dystrophin as you can see  the blank slide and the crisper gold  treated mice  crisper gold treated mdx mice you see a  lot of dystrophin positivity  in the vicinity of the injection and in  some cases it's as high as 60  of the muscle fiber so we do appear to  be  inducing the re-expression of the  dystrophin protein  one of the benefits of crispr gold is  that it delivers the cas9 protein itself  which should get rapidly degraded after  the editing has occurred  and this should lead to a low amount of  off-target dna damage  so we we measured this by doing deep  sequencing  on uh on mice on mdx mice that had been  treated with crispr gold  where we sequenced six sites in the  genome that had a very high homology to  our guide rna  the results of this deep sequencing is  shown here and you can see that  uh in general our off-target dna damage  is extremely low it's actually below  uh the level of deep sequencing accuracy  so these are all  under one percent the highest one was  point one seven percent which is barely  at the threshold of of uh  of being above background and uh this is  certainly much lower than what you would  see  with virally delivered castling where  generally the off-target dna damage  is in the one to two percent level  finally we investigated if crispr gold  could improve muscle function  in the mdx mass model so in this  experiment we treated mdx mice with  crispr gold designed to correct the  dystrophin mutation  and then we did a four limb hanging test  to see how long the mice could hang  upside down  on a barbed wire mesh and the results  are shown here the y-axis  is the hanging time the x-axis is the  various groups  and what you can see here is that crispr  gold treated mice had a two-fold  increase  in hanging time or muscle function over  the control mice  they're still below the wild type but  this is certainly a trend in the right  direction  so based on these experiments um um  the the lead author of this work uh was  a  was a scientist named lee he was a  graduate student in my lab he formed a  company  uh based on this project called gen edit  he's the ceo of gen edit  and um i don't have any equity in  genetics so i've been so i can  collaborate with gen edit without any  problem and what i'm going to talk about  in the next few slides is a  collaboration that we've been doing  that was done in in partnership with gen  edit  so there are numerous organs where gene  editing can have therapeutic effects  of these the brain is perhaps one of the  most exciting  and this is because neurological  diseases like alzheimer's and  huntington's affect millions of people  every year their incidence is increasing  and there's a great unmet medical need  and new therapeutics are very urgently  needed  in addition developing therapeutics for  neurological diseases  is inherently challenging because of the  formula or movemental barrier of the  blood-brain barrier  and also because uh if you just globally  inhibit neuronal signaling  pathways that results in a lot of  toxicity  we think that localized gene editing  accomplished via an intracranial  injection has tremendous therapeutic  potential  because it overcomes the problem of the  blood-brain barrier  and in addition if you do an  intracranial injection  of gene editing enzymes you will only  edit the region of the tissue where you  inject it and you will thereby spare  the toxic effects of of globally  inhibiting neuronal signaling pathways  and finally because gene editing is  permanent you only have to do this once  and intracranial injections are already  done  in a variety of clinical scenarios  however despite its potential  very little is known about gene editing  in the brain  so we therefore started a collaboration  with jen edit and professor hey young  lee  hey young is a professor at the  university of texas in san antonio  and we investigated if crispr gold could  do gene editing in the brain for these  experiments we use the ai9 mouse model  the ai9 mouse is shown mouse model is  shown here and it's a transgenic mouse  that has the td tomato gene upstream of  a stop coda  so ai9 mice do not normally make the red  fluorescent protein  however because of the stop codon  however if you edit out the stop  sequences  using crispr you can then get the turn  on of red fluorescence  so um gen edit and us we made crispr  gold that was designed to do this  editing  and uh they figured out how to how to  manufacture and ship it and they shipped  it to  the university of texas in san antonio  and hey young then  injected it into the striatum and  hippocampus of ai9  mice and then looked for red  fluorescence and so the results are  shown on the next slide here  so on this slide every every every  little red dot is a cell that has been  that has been cut twice with cast iron  and had a recombination event  that led to uh the elimination of the  stop signal  and then the the the expression of the  td tomato or red fluorescent protein  so you can see in the cast nine case uh  this is this is the  the mice treated with crispr gold with  cas9 you can see a lot of little red  dots  each one of those is an edited cell uh  the control uninjected you don't see any  red  and so this demonstrates that crispr  gold can do gene ending in the brain  for these experiments we also made  crispr gold complex to cpf1 and hey  young injected it in the striatum and  again she saw  a lot of red fluorescence demonstrating  successful editing  hey young was able to get the percentage  of cells that had been edited in the  brain by  doing a simultaneous dappy staining and  then comparing the red  versus the blue so that's what this  looks like here  and was then able to get the percentage  of cell edited edited and what we saw in  both cases with crispr gold and cast  iron and crispr gold with cpf1  is that we're editing around 10 of the  cells and this is actually a fairly  challenging edit to do because for a  cell to be rfp positive  it has to receive two cuts of crispr and  then also recombine  and it turns out the maximum you can get  in this animal model is about 25  so 10 is actually a fairly efficient  amount of editing  based on this hey young investigated if  crispr gold could  edit neurons and for these experiments  she used a transgenic mouse model that  expresses the yfp gene only in neurons  we made crispr gold or c crispr gold  with either cas9 or cpf1 that was  designed to knock out the yfp gene  she injected it into the hippocampus and  then looked for  if there was a decrease in the number of  yfp positive cells  the results are shown here the y-axis is  the percentage of yfp positive cells and  the x-axis  is the control which is untreated or  crispr gold  and i should mention here that to to  make a cell yfp negative all you have to  do is cut it once so this is a much  easier type of edit than the  ai9 mouse model where you have to get  two cuts and also a recombination so  here you just need one cut  and so our efficiency is actually much  higher here so we're get so  wild type is up to about 30 of the cells  in histology slide whereas if you treat  it with crystal  crispr gold this goes down to about 10  so almost a two-thirds reduction  and we saw similar things with cpf1 so  crispr gold can do editing  of neurons and it's fairly efficient at  knocking out genes  so we therefore investigated if we could  have therapeutic effects in treating  brain diseases using crispr gold  the first disease we've looked at is  fragile x-related autism  fragile x-related autism is the most  common hereditary form of autism  and it's a disease caused by a triplet  repeat expansion  in the fmr1 gene and  this causes the inactivation of the fmr1  gene or the fmr1 protein  the fm1 protein is a is an  rna binding protein that acts as a  global inhibitor  of protein expression  and there's currently no effective  treatment for fmr1 related autism  and there's therefore great interest in  developing new therapeutic strategies  now it's also known that in fmr1 uh  related autism  that um um m glue r5  that the m2r5 signaling pathway is over  activated  the mgu r5 signaling pathway is shown on  this slide here and blue r5 is an is  an ion it's an ion channel and it plays  an essential role  in learning and memory and when the m2r5  signaling pathway gets activated  uh that results in a large amount of  protein expression  and the protein expression is an  essential part of mlr5 signaling  now it turns out that in a healthy  patient or in a healthy mouse  when you get mlr5 signaling the fmr1  protein  acts as an inhibitor of acts as an  inhibitor of mgr5 signaling  because it will shut down all the  proteins that are being expressed  due to m2r5 signaling and therefore acts  as a break on angular 5 sigma  however if you have fragile x syndrome  um  which is caused by basically  inactivation of the fmr1 protein  so if you have fragile x syndrome then  you don't have the fmr1 protein and when  you get mlr5 signaling  that signaling goes undamped and is and  and continues to propagate long after it  should  because the f1 protein is not there to  shut down the proteins  uh the protein synthesis induced by mglu  r5 sigma  so there's therefore great interest in  being able to develop inhibitors for  mlr5  and and drug companies have spent a lot  of time trying to do this however this  has been challenging  because uh primarily because of toxicity  problems mdr5  is an essential protein a variety of  other contexts and if you just globally  shut it down there'll be toxicity  and it also has very similar homology to  and also a lot of other ion channels  that have homology to it so it's very  hard to selectively knock out mlr5  in addition the blood-brain barrier is a  challenge  so we therefore investigated if we could  use crispr gold  to knock out m blue r5 in  fmr1 knockout mice and also in wild type  mice and rescue mice from fragile x  related autism  so we made crispr gold that was designed  to do this we shipped it to hey young's  lab  and she injected it into the striatum  and hippocampus  and looked for the numbers of f of of  mlr5  negative cells she did this by  immunohistochemistry  and um and um and the results are shown  here  so the y-axis is the percent of mgr five  positive cells  and the x-axis is the various groups and  uh what you can see here is that both in  the wild-type case  and in the fmr1 knockout case you see  about a 50  reduction in the amount of m2r5 cells  after treatment with push for gold  demonstrating that crispr gold can knock  out ml5 in the brain after  intracranial injection based on this we  investigated  if we could rescue mice from some of the  behavioral phenotypes that are caused  from that that are observed in the fm1  knockout mass  so it turns out that the fm1 knockout  mouse exhibits many of the repetitive  behaviors  that are characteristic of autism uh one  of them  is obsessive burying so if you take the  effort and knockout mice and put them  in a cage with marbles and sand  they will bury all the marbles and  we investigated if knocking out m2r5  could rescue this phenotype  results are shown here the y-axis is the  percentage of marbles buried  x-axis is the various groups so you can  see that in the control fm1 knockout  mice they bury more  more marbles than the wild type mice  and that the amount of marbles that are  buried decreases  if you knock out mlr5 in the stride i'm  using crisper gold  so here you see a lot of marbles and  here all the marbles are buried  in addition we also were able to rescue  against some of the other phenotypes  observed in the fm1 knockout mice one of  them  is is the jumping phenotype so it turns  out that fmr1 knockout mice  jump up and down obsessively that's  shown here this is the amount of jumps  for  in a cage the y axis is the jumps this  is the control  wild type mice and this is the control  fm1 knockout mice you see of almost a  20-fold increase in the amount of jumps  however if you knock out mgu r5 in the  striatum  with crisper gold you get a big  reduction in the in the amount of jumps  i'm now going to switch gears and talk  about some other delivery strategies  that are  that have been developed in the lab and  the first one is focused on  improving the gene editing activity and  delivery of  cpf1 cpf1 is  a fairly recent rna guided endonuclease  that has emerged as a very exciting  alternative or complement to cas9  and so cpf1 what makes it uh  interesting is that it has a different  pam  recognition sequence which is t rich as  opposed to cas9 which has a g-rich  pam sequence and what this means is that  cpf1 can edit regions of the genome that  cas9 cannot in addition  uh cpf1 generates sticky ends when it  cuts dna  and that may lead to better hdr and  certainly could change the outcome of  editing events  after dna repair and especially in  comparison to cas9 which generates  uh blunt ants and finally  cpf1 has a 40 base crrna which is much  smaller than the 100 pace  guide rna of cast iron so make  manufacturing the guide rna in  the case of cpf1 will be much easier now  in the case of cas9 it was known that  that modification and optimization of  the guide rna led to large increases in  the gene editing activity of cas9  however similar optimizations and  chemical modifications of the cpf1 crna  have not been done yet  we therefore investigated if we could  increase the gene editing activity and  delivery of cpf1  by engineering its guide rna and what we  discovered was that if you take the cpf1  crna  and extend it at the five prime end you  can increase its gene editing activity  and delivery into cells  uh in three ways so the first  observation was that if you extend this  the crna at the five prime end uh the  absolute gene editing rate in cells  after electroporation of the rnp is  dramatically increased  and this leads to enhanced gene editing  in addition uh we can increase the serum  stability of the cpf1 crna because the  because the crna extension uh is  amenable to chemical modifications  which can stabilize it against nucleases  whereas the wild type crna is not  and so you can enhance the serum  stability because you can now chemically  modify the five prime end  and finally if you just extend the cpf1  crna  the negative charge density of the rnp  formed  after complexing cpf1 is dramatically  increased  and that leads to more efficient  complexation with cationic delivery  vectors and more efficient delivery in  vivo  so in this experiment we investigated if  we could increase the gene editing  activity of cpf1 by extending the five  prime  end so we so we purchased cpf1 crnas  that had various extensions ranging from  59 to four  base extensions and uh these crnas  targeted the gps the gfp  gene uh we made rnps with them and  electroporated them into gfp hex cells  and then did flow cytometry and got the  percentage  that were gfp negative results are shown  here  this is the wild type crrna no  modification gives you about 20  gives you about 20 to 30 25 to 30  percent gfp minus  and that almost that dramatically  increases almost by twofold so we're now  almost at sixty percent with the various  modifications  so extending it by four nine fifteen and  twenty five all led to dramatic  enhancements  in gene editing in addition we also  investigated if uh these these  extensions  would tolerate chemical modification so  wild type cpf1  is does not tolerate modifications of  the five prime end but  uh if you extend it the extension uh  appears to and this was demonstrated  here  where we took the uh the four and nine  base extensions and we modified the  extensions with omethoxy phosphothio8  and deoxy and then looked to see if we  still saw an enhancement in the gene  editing activity of cpf1  uh the results are shown here the y-axis  is gfp negative cells the x-axis is the  various groups  so this is the wild type at around 25  and again the  the four base extension versus the four  base extension that has methoxy and  phosphothyroids are about the same  and again this is again a two-fold  increase so we can now do chemical  modifications  of the cpf5 primary and we have been  able to show that this leads to enhanced  stability and serum and finally we  we also investigated if we could um  use the extensions to uh to enhance  delivery  so we made the cpf1 rnp with a 59 base  extension so it's not very negatively  charged and  complex as cationic polymers and then we  delivered  this complex into the muscle tissue of  ai9 mice  and then looked for editing so again ai9  mice turn red when  when you when they're gene edited and so  we did an injection of the muscle tissue  and analyzed  six millimeters away from the injection  site and as you can see from these  slides that there is a lot of gene  editing happening  uh up to six millimeters away so we  think that the extension will also  enhance delivery by making it more  negatively charged  in the remaining few minutes i want to  talk about another strategy for  delivering the cas9 rnp  which is focused on making cas9 rnp  peptide conjugates  and uh we think that cas9 rnp peptide  conjugates have a lot of potential  or and certainly some benefits over  nanoparticle based technologies as i  presented earlier  and this is because a cas9 peptide  conjugate is going to be much smaller  it's going to be about 10 to 15  nanometers versus nanoparticles which  are generally hundreds to 200  of nanometers and because of their  smaller size  they should be able to diffuse and edit  tissues that are going to be  inaccessible by nanoparticles  however a major challenge with making  cas9  rnp peptide conjugates is the fact that  if you start chemically modifying the  cast iron rnp you will most likely  uh kill its its nuclease activity so to  overcome this problem uh we came up with  a linker called  trailer which is shown here which is a  disulfide  of linker that allows you to chemically  modify the lysine residues of a protein  but then once it goes inside of a cell  and sees the the  glutathione inside of a cell this  glutathione then triggers  a self-immolation reaction which causes  the release of the unmodified protein  so the chemical uh structure of the  trailer linker is shown here it's  composed of two  disulfide sulfurs an ethyl carbonate  linker and a one six elec a 1 6  elimination  linker connected in series this is the  drug and this is the delivery vector  and once you break this bond you get two  consecutive self-immolation reactions  which leads to rapid and quantitative  release  of the amine and this and if it's a  protein it's going to be the lysine  residue  so we made this reagent here called cpp  trailer which is composed of  uh the cell penetrating peptide arginine  9  connected to a fitzy residue for for uh  for tracking and  also the trailer linker connected to a  paranitro  uh carbamate shown here so that you  could react it with proteins in one step  so we've made cas9 conjugated to this  trailer cpp  and we've investigated what's the  release kinetics uh  after after incubation in 10 millimolar  glutathione  the results are shown here the y-axis is  the cas9 cpp percent  the x-axis is time and you can see that  in in 10 millimolar glutathione the  half-life is about five minutes  this was done by by gel electrophoresis  where we're measuring the fitzy tag  we've also analyzed the um the  nucleus activity of this cas9 cpp  conjugate before and after reduction  the y-axis is nuclease activity the  x-axis is the various groups  cas9-cpp by itself has very low nuclease  activity and this is because  the cpps are randomly conjugated to  lysine residues and will most likely  inhibit  the enzyme function because several of  the lysines are in the active site  however if you reduce cast iron tpp with  gs8 you get  nuclease activity that's essentially  indistinguishable  from the wild type cas9 demonstrating  that we can actually release  active after reduction finally we've  investigated if we could do gene editing  with cas9 cpp  so in this experiment we made cas9 cpp  designed to cut the gfp gene  and incubate it with gfp hex cells and  then measure the amount of gfp  knockout by sanger sequencing the  results are shown here  and the y-axis is editing efficiency  this is the cast nine  this is the cells that have been treated  with cas9 cvp and we're at about five  percent editing and is much higher than  the wild type cast 9 added to cells  which is barely at baseline levels  so in conclusion i've talked about two  different delivery vectors for  transducing the cas9 rnp in vivo the  first one was called crispr gold  and the second one was based off of um  making peptide conjugates with the cas9  rnp  or cpp trailer finally i'd like to thank  the people that did the work  all of the christopher goldwork was done  by one very talented graduate student  named kumuli who's now the ceo of gen  edit the the cpp trailer work  was done by a postdoc named mao maohi  who's shown here  and then i also want to thank all of our  collaborators  were shown here and finally i'd like to  thank you for your attention
 

